Title: Derive each deliverable's corpus snapshot date from the artifact, not a test literal
Created: 2026-07-27
Author: HDMX-coding-agent

--- log ---
2026-07-27T07:54Z HDMX-coding-agent created
2026-07-27T08:00Z HDMX-coding-agent note found while clearing make check-fast for the RDJ-26561 resubmission (ticket 0317): test_snapshot_date_documented had a single hardcoded SNAPSHOT_DATE = 2026-03-26 asserted against all three documents, and had been red on main since the data paper's as-of date moved to 2026-07-24 (commit 6faa0876). Unblocked with per-document literals; this ticket is the real fix.

--- body ---
## Context

Three deliverables state the corpus snapshot their numbers come from, in
prose. `tests/test_snapshot_date_documented.py` asserted one shared literal
against all three, so the moment the data paper was rebuilt on corpus v2 the
test went red and stayed red — a stale guard reporting a stale fact, with no
way to tell which of the two had moved.

The documents are legitimately on different corpora:

- `deliverables/data-paper/data-paper.qmd` — corpus v2, 2026-07-24
- `deliverables/multilayer/multilayer-detection.qmd` — v1, 2026-03-26
- `deliverables/corpus-report/corpus-report.qmd` — v1, 2026-03-26

Per-document literals unblock CI but keep the number hand-maintained in two
places (the prose and the test), which is the defect class this repo already
rules against: no hand-curated data in the pipeline, and vars-driven numbers
in prose.

## Relevant files

- `tests/test_snapshot_date_documented.py` — the per-document literals to retire
- `scripts/analysis/compute_vars.py` — where a `corpus_snapshot_date` var belongs
- `data/catalogs/run_reports/catalog_merge__*.json` — carries the real build
  timestamp, but is DVC-managed and absent in a clean CI checkout

## Actions

1. Emit `corpus_snapshot_date` as a computed var from the corpus build report,
   into each deliverable's `-vars.yml`.
2. Replace the prose literals with `{{< meta corpus_snapshot_date >}}` in the
   documents built on the current corpus.
3. For documents deliberately pinned to an older corpus (multilayer,
   corpus-report on v1), pin the var in their `-vars.yml` the way
   `manuscript-vars.yml` is pinned, and say so in a comment.
4. Rewrite the test to assert the prose date equals the document's own var —
   a consistency check, not a literal.

## Test

- Red first: mutate one document's `-vars.yml` snapshot date and assert the
  test fails naming that document.
- The test must pass in a checkout with no `data/catalogs/` present (CI has
  no corpus data), so it reads the committed `-vars.yml`, never the corpus.

## Verification

- [ ] `make check-fast` green with no corpus data on disk
- [ ] Changing the corpus and re-running `make stats` moves the data paper's
      documented date with no test edit

## Invariants

- No hand-typed corpus numbers in prose (project rule).
- The check keeps failing when a document's prose and its corpus disagree —
  this ticket makes the guard sharper, never looser.

## Exit criteria

- [ ] Snapshot date is vars-driven in every document not deliberately pinned
- [ ] `tests/test_snapshot_date_documented.py` holds no date literals
